Title: ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION fails intermittently on a broken pipe
Status: open
Found: 2026-09-06, in passing, running the suite for an unrelated change

SYMPTOM

    ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION in CLAUTOLISP-TOOL-SUITE:
      Unexpected Error: #<SB-INT:BROKEN-PIPE ...>
      Couldn't write to #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for
        "socket 127.0.0.1:44119, peer: 127.0.0.1:46746">:
        Broken pipe.

One failure out of 33 suites. The IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWING run of the same
suite, same image, same tree, was green: 33 suites, 0 failures. Nothing
between the two runs changed.

WHY IT IS WORTH A TICKET RATHER THAN A SHRUG

A test that fails once in a while is worse than one that fails always.
It teaches people to re-run rather than to look, and the day it fails
for a REAL reason it will be re-run too. This project has already spent
days on the opposite failure -- a silence read as evidence -- and a
flake is the same coin: an outcome nobody trusts enough to act on.

It also lands in CI, where a red pipeline on a merge request costs a
maintainer's attention before anyone knows it was noise.

WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE

The test drives a live aldb listener over a loopback socket. A broken
pipe on write means the peer closed first -- so the likely shape is a
race between the client finishing (or erroring) and the server writing
its next line, rather than anything about the protocol's content. Under
load, which is when a full suite run is at its slowest, the window
widens.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: run that test alone in a loop, a few hundred
times, and see whether the failure rate matches the load hypothesis.
Cheap, since it needs no CAD and no runner.

WHAT NOT TO DO: mark it flaky and retry it. That converts a race into a
slower race and keeps the untrustworthy outcome. Either the close is
ordered and the test should wait for it, or the writer should tolerate
a closed peer as a normal end -- and which of those is right is a
statement about the listener, not about the test.

NOT INTRODUCED BY THE COMPILER BRANCH: it appeared while running the
suite for a documentation change, on a tree whose only code delta was
one new compiler test, and the listener is master's work. Recorded here
rather than in that branch's issues because it belongs to the debugger
UI, not the compiler.
